# Agent Workspace Hygiene

Multiple agents work this repo in parallel. Without discipline the
primary checkout silently becomes a pile of everyone's half-finished
edits: files that match no branch, stashes nobody can date, and
duplicate copies left behind by refactors. That state is not just
untidy — it hides real uncommitted work, and it makes "is my change
safe?" unanswerable without hashing every file against every branch.

## One task, one worktree

- Do not build a feature in the primary checkout at
  `/Users/logosrx/Desktop/Pharmax` while another agent may be working.
  Create a dedicated worktree:
  `git worktree add ../Pharmax-<task> -b <branch>`
- Remove the worktree when the branch merges:
  `git worktree remove ../Pharmax-<task>`
- Never leave a worktree pointing at a branch that has been merged or
  deleted. A stale worktree also blocks `git branch -d`.

## Leave the tree the way you found it

- Before ending a task, every file you touched must be either committed
  and pushed, or deliberately reverted. "Modified but pushed elsewhere"
  is not a resting state.
- Run `git status --short` as your last step. If it lists files you
  created or modified, resolve them before reporting done.
- Shared merge-point files — `prisma/schema.prisma`,
  `packages/events/src/registry.ts`, `packages/rbac/src/permissions.ts`,
  `packages/database/src/index.ts`, `pnpm-lock.yaml` — are touched by
  almost every feature. Leaving your edit to one of these in the tree
  silently contaminates the next agent's branch. Commit it to your
  branch or revert it.

## Stashes are not a filing system

- Do not use `git stash` as a "safety snapshot" before pushing. A stash
  has no branch, no message discipline, and no owner; three days later
  nobody can tell whether it holds unique work.
- If you need a checkpoint, make a commit on a branch and push it. A
  WIP commit on a throwaway branch is recoverable and attributable; a
  stash is neither.
- If you do create a stash, drop it in the same task.

## Moving a file means moving it

- When relocating a module, delete the original in the same step. An
  untracked byte-identical copy at the old path resolves for nobody,
  survives every branch, and reads like unfinished work forever.
- After a move, `rg` for the old import path and confirm zero hits.

## Don't let green fixes rot

- A PR that is green and fixes a repo-wide failure blocks every other
  PR's signal until it merges. Land it or close it — do not leave it
  open overnight.
- Before opening a new PR against a red baseline, check whether the
  failure is already fixed in an open PR. Duplicating the fix wastes a
  review; waiting behind it wastes a day.

## Deploys are manual and can silently roll back

- `deploy.yml` is `workflow_dispatch` only. Merging to `main` does not
  ship. Never assume `main` and production are the same code.
- The `production` GitHub Environment requires a reviewer. A dispatched
  deploy sits in `waiting` until a human approves it — check for a
  stalled run before dispatching another.
- ECS deployment circuit breaker rolls a service back on boot failure,
  and the matrix deploys services independently. A partial failure
  leaves production running **mixed versions**. After any deploy,
  confirm the running image tag of every service, not just the one you
  cared about.
